# tapefuse

tapefuse is a Linux userspace daemon and CLI tool that presents a FUSE
filesystem backed by a real or emulated LTO tape drive using the
[LTFS](https://www.ibm.com/docs/en/ts4300-tape-lib?topic=formats-linear-tape-file-system-ltfs)
(Linear Tape File System) format.

---

## Features

- **LTFS 2.4.0** index/label format (XML, fully spec-compatible)
- **File-backed simulation** — creates `.p0.dat` / `.p1.dat` partition images on
  any filesystem; no tape drive required for development and testing
- **Real SCSI tape** — reads and writes LTO and MHVTL virtual tape drives on
  Linux using raw `MTIOCTOP` ioctls via `/dev/nst*`
- **FUSE filesystem** — once a tape is loaded and mounted, the contents appear
  as a normal directory; create, read, rename and delete operations are fully
  supported
- **Realtime file I/O** — file data is buffered per open file handle (in memory
  or in a temporary file under `/tmp/ltape/buffer/`, depending on the mount
  option) and written to tape as a **single large block** when the file
  descriptor is closed.  This matches the behaviour of LTFS and LTFSCopyGUI:
  writing one block per file maximises streaming performance and avoids the
  inter-block gaps that would result from writing many small 128 KB blocks (one
  per FUSE write chunk).  Reads go directly to tape; during an open write the
  data is served from the buffer.  Two flush strategies keep tape use efficient:
  - *New / truncated file*: the complete buffer is appended at the data
    partition EOD as a single block on close.
  - *Overwrite existing file*: the current tape content is pre-loaded into the
    buffer on the first write, the writes are applied, and the merged result is
    written back as a single block on close (old extents are freed).
- **Configurable write buffers** — `ltape mount -f0 …` (default) uses a
  temporary file in `/tmp/ltape/buffer/`; `-m0` uses heap memory; a size
  suffix caps the per-file buffer (e.g. `-f4G`, `-m1G`); `0` means no limit.
  File-backed buffers keep large write-staging data off the Go heap and let the
  OS page it under memory pressure.
- **Immediate flush** — `ltape flushfiles <letter>` flushes all currently-open
  dirty file buffers to tape without waiting for the file descriptors to close,
  useful for transferring files that remain open for a long time.
- **Disk-cached index** — the LTFS index XML is never held only in RAM; it is
  read from and written to `/tmp/ltape/index/<letter>.index` on every metadata
  operation, so the cache is always consistent and survives daemon crashes
- **Explicit commit** — the LTFS index must be committed separately with
  `ltape commit` (or by writing to `/.tapefuse_flush`); this mirrors how real
  tape drives work and prevents index bloat
- **Sparse space reuse** — deleted-file blocks are recorded as available space
  in the index and reused on subsequent writes (best-fit selection)
- **Defrag** — `ltape defrag` compacts the data partition by staging live files
  to disk, truncating at the first gap, and rewriting them sequentially
- **Physical eject** — `ltape eject` unmounts the FUSE filesystem, waits for
  the FUSE goroutine to exit, and issues the SCSI `MTOFFL` command to
  physically retract the tape before removing the assignment
- **Prefix abbreviation** — all `ltape` commands support unambiguous prefix
  matching: `ltape mo a /mnt` resolves to `mount`; an ambiguous prefix like
  `d` prints `ambiguous: "d" matches daemon, defrag, discard`
- **Verbose operation logs** — `ltaped` logs daemon lifecycle and command
  handling; `ltape` prints request/response progress to stderr

