## Backends

### File-backed (`internal/tape.Partition`)

The default mode when `<device>` is not a character device.  Two regular
files (`<device>.p0.dat`, `<device>.p1.dat`) store records using a simple
binary framing:

```
[4 bytes LE] record type  (0=data, 1=filemark, 0xFFFF=EOD)
[4 bytes LE] data length
[N bytes]    data
```

An in-memory block index (`[]int64`) maps block numbers to byte offsets for
O(1) random access and O(1) truncation.  This backend is fully portable and
is the primary target for unit tests.

### SCSI tape (`internal/tape.SCSITape`, Linux only)

When `<device>` is detected as a character device (`os.Stat` mode
`ModeCharDevice`), the daemon opens `/dev/nst<N>` (no-rewind variant) and
uses Linux tape ioctls via `golang.org/x/sys/unix`:

| ioctl `MTIOCTOP` op | Used for |
|---------------------|----------|
| `MTREW`  | Rewind to BOT before reads and before writing index |
| `MTFSF n` | Space forward n filemarks (seeking past filemark blocks) |
| `MTFSR n` | Space forward n data records (seeking past data blocks) |
| `MTWEOF n` | Write n filemarks |
| `MTEOM`  | Seek to logical end of medium (EOD) for reliable write positioning |
| `MTOFFL` | Rewind and eject the tape cartridge |

`SCSITape` maintains a `blockTypes []RecordType` slice (populated by a
sequential scan on `OpenSCSI`) that records whether each block is a data
record, an ordinary filemark, or the EOD filemark (`RecordEOD`).  This is
used to choose the correct space command when seeking to an arbitrary block
number.  When writing, the tape head is positioned at the physical EOD via
`MTEOM` (rather than rewinding and forward-spacing across the double-FM EOD
marker, which would fail on real drives and MHVTL).

A `SCSIRegionTape` wrapper provides the index (offset 0) and data (offset 6)
views that the `TapeFS` layer expects.

Non-Linux builds compile in a stub (`scsi_stub.go`) that returns an error from
`OpenSCSI` / `CreateSCSI`, so the package builds cleanly everywhere.

